WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Start with the chloroplast schematic, and follow each leader line to its

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.320
endpoint before naming a structure.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.100
Label A brackets a stack of flattened sacs: one stack is a granum.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.440
B points to a membrane of one sac, a thylakoid.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:17.360
C marks the surrounding internal fluid region, the stroma.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:21.660
D indicates the chloroplast envelope, shown as two boundary lines.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:25.960
The drawing is not to scale and omits many molecular details.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:30.800
A bracket around several sacs does not mean one thylakoid, and the stroma is

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.360
not the space inside a thylakoid.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:32.760
Pause and explain all four labels without using the answer options.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:36.652
Keep the same chloroplast diagram and turn the labels into functional

00:00:36.652 --> 00:00:37.160
evidence.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:42.100
Light dependent electron transport occurs on the thylakoid membrane indicated

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:42.680
by B.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.100
Calvin cycle reactions occur in the stroma indicated by C.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:53.240
ATP and NADPH from the light reactions support carbon reduction, but a

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.572
structure drawing alone does not tell you the actual rate in a particular

00:00:56.572 --> 00:00:56.800
leaf.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.260
The double envelope at D is a separate membrane system from the thylakoids.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:06.720
If a question swaps the words stroma and granum, test the location rather than

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:07.500
memorising the letter.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.820
Letters are local to this schematic and can be reassigned in another question.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:16.460
Read the circulation diagram as a connectivity map rather than an anatomical

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:16.880
portrait.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:23.480
The arrows run from body to A, then B, then lungs, then C, then D and back to

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:23.720
body.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:29.180
In the standard adult human circulation, A is the right atrium and B the right

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:29.560
ventricle.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:33.220
C is the left atrium and D the left ventricle.

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:37.420
A and C are receiving chambers; B and D pump blood out of the heart.

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:41.320
Their positions on the screen do not establish anatomical left and right.

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:44.080
Follow where blood comes from and where it goes.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:49.080
This diagram deliberately prioritises the route over realistic chamber shapes.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:52.340
Use the same route to avoid a common naming error.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:57.429
An artery carries blood away from the heart; a vein carries it toward the

00:01:57.429 --> 00:01:57.760
heart.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:00.580
Oxygen content is not the definition.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:05.480
In the standard adult pulmonary circuit, the artery leaving B carries

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:10.540
relatively deoxygenated blood toward the lungs, while pulmonary veins return

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:12.360
oxygenated blood to C.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.980
D pumps into the systemic circuit.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:19.169
The arrows show connectivity and direction, not a measured pressure

00:02:19.169 --> 00:02:19.780
difference,

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:21.980
blood speed or cardiac output.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:26.480
If a question asks for one of those quantities, it must provide additional

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:27.060
information.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:32.540
First identify the chamber and circuit, then classify the vessel by its

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.080
direction relative to the heart.

00:02:34.344 --> 00:02:36.484
Now read the embryo sac schematic.

00:02:37.344 --> 00:02:41.444
The chalazal end is at the top and the micropylar end at the bottom.

00:02:42.384 --> 00:02:45.524
A identifies the three antipodal cells at the chalazal end.

00:02:46.344 --> 00:02:51.544
C points to the egg at the micropylar end, and D identifies the two flanking

00:02:51.544 --> 00:02:52.144
synergids.

00:02:52.944 --> 00:02:56.104
B marks the two polar nuclei in the large central cell.

00:02:56.644 --> 00:02:59.544
The egg and synergids together form the egg apparatus.

00:03:00.484 --> 00:03:04.524
Do not decide the identity from top and bottom alone, because another drawing

00:03:04.524 --> 00:03:05.304
may be rotated.

00:03:06.084 --> 00:03:10.724
This figure specifies the common Polygonum type pattern before polar nuclear

00:03:10.724 --> 00:03:15.404
fusion; it is not a claim that every flowering plant has exactly this

00:03:15.404 --> 00:03:15.704
arrangement.

00:03:16.296 --> 00:03:21.416
Count the six small cells in the drawing: three antipodals, two synergids and

00:03:21.416 --> 00:03:21.836
one egg.

00:03:22.536 --> 00:03:25.216
Add the large central cell to obtain seven cells.

00:03:25.956 --> 00:03:30.411
Before polar nuclear fusion, the central cell contains two nuclei, so the

00:03:30.411 --> 00:03:30.716
total

00:03:30.716 --> 00:03:31.716
is eight nuclei.

00:03:32.516 --> 00:03:37.136
During normal double fertilisation, one sperm fuses with the egg at C to form

00:03:37.136 --> 00:03:38.136
the diploid zygote.

00:03:38.636 --> 00:03:42.956
The other combines with the two haploid polar nuclei at B, or their already

00:03:42.956 --> 00:03:47.196
fused secondary nucleus, to form the typical triploid primary endosperm

00:03:47.196 --> 00:03:47.636
nucleus.

00:03:48.496 --> 00:03:51.896
Count cells separately from nuclei, and state the developmental stage.

00:03:52.476 --> 00:03:56.636
A change in nuclear fusion does not automatically imply another cell division.

00:03:56.904 --> 00:04:02.824
The final schematic shows grass at A, grasshopper at B, frog at C and snake

00:04:02.824 --> 00:04:03.404
at D.

00:04:04.144 --> 00:04:07.144
Read the arrows as energy transfer from food to consumer.

00:04:07.804 --> 00:04:13.124
A is the producer, B the primary consumer, C the secondary consumer and D

00:04:13.124 --> 00:04:15.804
the tertiary consumer in this stated grazing chain.

00:04:16.164 --> 00:04:20.864
The drawing is a simplified model: real diets can form food webs, and

00:04:20.864 --> 00:04:23.404
decomposers participate even though they are not drawn.

00:04:23.864 --> 00:04:27.864
Do not interpret a missing box as proof that a process does not occur in

00:04:27.864 --> 00:04:28.024
nature.

00:04:28.944 --> 00:04:32.764
Identify the trophic level from the given feeding relationship, not just the

00:04:32.764 --> 00:04:33.944
animal's name in isolation.

00:04:34.272 --> 00:04:38.712
For this calculation only, apply ten percent transfer at each boundary.

00:04:39.712 --> 00:04:44.027
Starting with ten thousand kilojoules at A gives one thousand at B, one

00:04:44.027 --> 00:04:44.452
hundred

00:04:44.452 --> 00:04:46.272
at C and ten at D.

00:04:46.712 --> 00:04:51.372
There are three transfers between A and D, so multiply by zero point one three

00:04:51.372 --> 00:04:51.932
times.

00:04:52.672 --> 00:04:56.512
If you compare production measurements, they must refer to matching areas and

00:04:56.512 --> 00:04:57.132
time periods.

00:04:57.952 --> 00:05:01.952
Ten percent is a stated teaching approximation, not an exact measured

00:05:01.952 --> 00:05:03.472
efficiency for every ecosystem.

00:05:04.112 --> 00:05:08.072
The energy not incorporated into the next level's production follows other

00:05:08.072 --> 00:05:12.012
pathways, including respiration, waste and uneaten material.

00:05:12.752 --> 00:05:15.372
It is not all stored as biomass in the next box.
